  "question": "Evaluate: $\\int_{1/3}^{1} \\frac{(x-x^3)^{1/3}}{x^4} dx$",
  "answeroption": "",
  "explanation": "**Step 1: Simplify the Integrand**  \n$$(x - x^3)^{1/3} = [x(1 - x^2)]^{1/3} = x^{1/3}(1 - x^2)^{1/3}$$  \nSo, the integrand becomes:  \n$$\\frac{x^{1/3}(1 - x^2)^{1/3}}{x^4} = \\frac{(1 - x^2)^{1/3}}{x^{11/3}}$$\n\n**Step 2: Apply Substitution**  \nLet $t = \\frac{1}{x^2} - 1 = x^{-2} - 1$. Then $dt = -2x^{-3} dx \\Rightarrow dx = -\\frac{x^3}{2} dt$.  \nAlso, $(1 - x^2)^{1/3} = x^{2/3}(x^{-2} - 1)^{1/3} = x^{2/3} t^{1/3}$.  \nSo, $\\frac{(1 - x^2)^{1/3}}{x^{11/3}} dx = \\frac{x^{2/3} t^{1/3}}{x^{11/3}} \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x^3}{2}\\right) dt = -\\frac{1}{2} t^{1/3} dt$.\n\n**Step 3: Change the Limits**  \nWhen $x = \\frac{1}{3}$: $t = \\frac{1}{(1/3)^2} - 1 = 9 - 1 = 8$  \nWhen $x = 1$: $t = 1 - 1 = 0$  \nSo, $I = -\\frac{1}{2} \\int_{8}^{0} t^{1/3} dt = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{8} t^{1/3} dt$\n\n**Step 4: Evaluate the Integral**  \n$$I = \\frac{1}{2} \\left[ \\frac{t^{4/3}}{4/3} \\right]_{0}^{8} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} [t^{4/3}]_{0}^{8} = \\frac{3}{8} (8^{4/3} - 0)$$  \n$$8^{4/3} = (2^3)^{4/3} = 2^4 = 16$$  \n$$I = \\frac{3}{8} \\times 16 = 6$$\n\n**Step 5: State the Final Answer**  \nThe value of the definite integral is $\\boxed{6}$.",
